  "# Which web architectures fit Kotoba, and how that was decided

## Context

The question asked was which architectures suit building a web framework or
library on today's Kotoba language surface. Answering it from memory would have
reproduced a failure this workspace has recorded repeatedly: quoting an
implementation ceiling as though it were a property of the language.

The root CLAUDE.md was itself behind. Read at origin/main on 2026-09-06:

- `kotoba-lang/kotoba-lang` 232182d
- `kotoba-lang/amu` 1e5b7b8f
- `kotoba-lang/kotoba-kir` af2275d

Each was fetched and confirmed equal to origin/main before being read, because a
west checkout is not the repository's main (ADR-2608136800).

Five things are landed that the prose in CLAUDE.md still described as absent or
as blockers:

1. `defrecord` / `defprotocol` / `extend-type` / `extend-protocol` are
   implemented, with profile `bounded-closed-world-static-dispatch`,
   `dynamic-fallback false`, and an unknown receiver rejected statically.
2. First-class closures are implemented: `fn`, `invoke`, `apply`, `fn-ref`,
   arity 0 to 4, capture plus parameter limit 5, with public `[:fn ...]`
   contracts crossing project-module boundaries.
3. `typed-eval` is `:implemented`. `(eval request)` takes a bounded document
   naming a checked DefCID, admitted on typed interface, effect row, allowed
   effects, fuel and max depth, with `:no-source-text` in its safety list.
4. `throw` and `try` exist (abort slice 2). An aborting function still cannot
   be exported, and throw is refused inside loop, doseq, dotimes, lazy thunks
   and fn literals, and in any function whose effect row carries a
   `:dataspace/*` operation.
5. `atom` / `swap!` / `reset!` / `deref` exist (local-state slice 1, decided
   2026-09-02). The cell may appear only as the first argument of those forms,
   in the body that binds it. Passing, returning, storing, capturing in a fn or
   reading inside an iteration form is refused by name.

Also read: the capability kits. `ui-v1` is a declarative flat tree of at most 32
nodes committed against a base revision with bounded pull for events;
`http-ingress-v1` is host-injects and guest-polls with one pending accept and a
queue of 8; `state-v1` is a host-owned key-value store of 256 entries whose
values are strings; `dataspace-v1` is assert, retract, observe and facets. Four
of the fourteen kits are qualified on `wasm-aot`, which is the browser target.

## Decision

### 1. Score against named invariants, not against taste

Seven axes, each bound to an invariant that is written down in the authority
files, with its disposition and shielding axis carried alongside:

| axis | invariant | disposition |
|---|---|---|
| authority | no-ambient-authority | intentional-security-constraint, code-identity |
| state | no-ambient-mutation | intentional-security-constraint, authority |
| dispatch | closed-world-static-dispatch | implemented-partial, dispatch-bypass |
| bounds | bounded-admission | intentional-security-constraint, resource-bounds |
| errors | explicit-errors | intentional-security-constraint, control-effect-tracking |
| concurrency | no-unbounded-concurrency | intentional-security-constraint, resource-bounds |
| identity | no-guest-macros | intentional-security-constraint, code-identity |

Recording the disposition matters more than recording the score. Three of these
have a widening path on file and four do not, so an architecture that fails on a
widening axis is waiting, while one that fails on a permanent axis has to be
rewritten.

### 2. Separate the facts from the judgement, and derive the number

Three artifacts, and no score is authored in any of them:

- `90-docs/architecture-fit/architecture-fit.datoms.edn` holds FACTS. Each
  architecture names one level per axis from a closed vocabulary, plus a
  first-party evidence URL and a note identifying the single fact that sets its
  lowest axis. `:source/dataset` is `architecture-fit`, so it joins the datom
  plane that `manifest/edn-query.cljs` already loads.
- `manifest/architecture-fit-rubric.edn` holds the JUDGEMENT: the level to score
  mapping, one table, applied uniformly.
- `scripts/architecture-fit-score.cljs` is the only place a total exists.

A disagreement is expressed by editing a level and recomputing. It cannot be
expressed by arguing with a printed number, because there is no printed number
to argue with that was not derived a moment ago.

### 3. The detector answers in three values, not two

Exit 0 clean, exit 1 the dataset disagrees with the rubric, exit 2 REFUSED. An
empty dataset returns 2, not 0: a scan of nothing is not a clean scan
(ADR-2608136000). Measured on 2026-09-06, all three fire:

- unmodified: exit 0, `SCANNED 57 entries`, clean
- one level changed to a keyword the rubric does not carry: exit 1, reported as
  `react-hooks unknown-level axis=state value=:totally-made-up`
- the exact string `:arch/errors :exception-control-flow ` deleted, which occurs
  once in the file: exit 1, reported as `rails missing-axis axis=errors`
- dataset replaced with `[]`: exit 2, REFUSED

The break and the report were compared each time. A first attempt at the second
case used a regex that spanned entities and produced seven findings with blank
entry names; that was a broken break rather than a successful demonstration, and
it was redone.

The check has already caught a real error: `:arch/entries` was hand-typed as 50
and the dataset holds 57. The first run refused to print a table.

## Findings

57 entries, mean 43.9, standard deviation 14.8, range 14 to 70, median 45.

### The binding constraint is bounds, not the security invariants

`bounds` has the lowest mean of any axis at 5.4, and is the lowest-scoring axis
for 12 of 57 entries, more than any other. The axes that get discussed --
ambient authority and ambient mutation -- bind 10 each.

This is the result worth carrying forward. Teams port an architecture expecting
to fight the capability model, and what actually stops them is max-parameters 5,
closure arity 4, ui-v1's 32 nodes, http-ingress-v1's single pending accept.
Those are numbers in files that move; the security invariants are not.

`dispatch` has the largest spread (sd 3.0), so it is the axis where the field
genuinely disagrees and therefore the one where the rubric is most worth
challenging.

### Family order, by mean total

managed-effect-ui 66.8 (n=4), component-model 66, coordination 64,
effect-description 62 (n=3), hypermedia 59, effect-typed 57.4 (n=5),
typed-server 50.5 (n=8), actor 46.5 (n=4), clojure 41 (n=5), continuation 38,
reactive-ui 35.7 (n=11), di 32.3 (n=7), plugin 21, mvc-orm 19.6 (n=5).

Two of these are worth naming.

**The client-side reactive family scores below the typed-server family and below
the actor family.** React, Reagent, Solid, Svelte, Vue, MobX and Lit average
35.7. The cause is the same in every case and it is not the rendering model: it
is a mutable cell held outside the function that reads it. `local-state.edn`
lists `captured-by-a-fn-literal` and `stored-in-a-collection-or-record` among
the refused escapes, which is a ratom and a `useState` cell described exactly.

**Dependency injection is the second-worst family.** This is the surprise, since
DI is normally the thing one adopts to get away from ambient authority, and the
authority axis does credit it. It is undone on dispatch and identity, because a
container resolves by name at run time. One measured fact settles it: a
capability handle is not a value in the source surface. `cap-acquire` and
`has-capability?` appear in the grammar's admitted builtins, but the frontend
answers that the operation has no admitted type signature and no admitted
lowering. There is nothing for a system map to hold.

Hexagonal ports-and-adapters at 52 is the exception inside that family, and the
difference is precisely that nothing is resolved by name.

### The top of the table is not a coincidence

Elm 68, Elmish 66, WASI 0.3 HTTP component 66, free monad interpreter 65,
Syndicate dataspace 64, CQRS event sourcing 63, Halogen 63, Unison 62.

Three of the top eight already exist as shipped capability kits or as the
compile target: `ui-v1` is TEA, `dataspace-v1` is Syndicate, and
`wasm-component-kotoba-v1` with WASI 0.3.0 is the component model. Two more --
free monad and CQRS -- describe the same move the capability invariant
describes: the unit produces a description and something with authority
interprets it.

HTMX at 59 is the highest-scoring client architecture in the table, and it
scores there by declining to be one. For a UI kit whose whole surface is
commit-a-tree and pull-events, hypermedia over the wire fits better than any
client-side reactive framework measured here.

Racket appears at both ends: Syndicate at 64 and the continuation-based
web-server at 38. A captured continuation is the unbounded non-inspectable
object that bounded admission cannot admit. The language is not the unit of fit.

## Consequences -- what a Kotoba web framework is

1. A handler, not a server. `http-ingress-v1` is synchronous accept-then-reply
   with one pending accept. Routing, concurrency and connection management are
   the host's.
2. Routes and middleware are literal values. `require`, `resolve`, `ns-resolve`,
   `var`, `load`, `load-file`, `load-string`, `read-string` and `binding` are
   forbidden heads, so a table assembled by scanning namespaces has no
   mechanism. reitit route data and a Pedestal interceptor chain are the two
   existing shapes that port unchanged.
3. The request is a record, not an argument list. max-parameters is 5 and
   `pure-product-profile.edn` states the policy outright as keep-5-use-records,
   with a preference order of record, option, hetero-vector, flat arity at most
   five. Record fields go to 32.
4. Handler abstraction is `defprotocol` plus `extend-protocol` over a closed set
   of records, resolved statically, with an unknown receiver refused at compile
   time.
5. The extension point is a DefCID. Typed eval is the only admitted way to run
   something not present at link time, and it names content rather than source.
   A plugin system that loads source text is not a thing to be worked around; it
   is a thing to be replaced with content addressing.
6. Boundaries return `[:result T E]`. throw exists but an aborting function
   cannot be exported.
7. The view is a 32-node flat tree committed against a base revision. That is
   optimistic concurrency over the whole tree, not a diff protocol, so there is
   nothing for a virtual-DOM differ to do.
8. State has three places, and which one is a counting question before it is a
   design question: a `let`-local cell that cannot escape, `state-v1` at 256
   entries with string values, or `dataspace-v1` at 256 assertions with facets.
   One global store holding everything fits none of them.

## What this does not claim

The facts are checkable against the cited first-party documents. The level
assigned to each fact is judgement, and the rubric turning a level into a number
is judgement. No gate produced these totals and none of them is a measurement.

The ranking is fit with one language surface. It is not a ranking of these
architectures as architectures, and a low score is not a defect in the thing
scored -- Rails at 14 is at the bottom because every convenience it is admired
for is the thing one of these seven invariants exists to refuse.

Twenty named frameworks were not covered: Ember, Nuxt, Remix, Sinatra, Flask,
Gin, Rocket, Vapor, Play, Micronaut, Quarkus, Deno Fresh, Astro, Nitro, Leptos,
Dioxus, Seaside, Ur/Web, Opa and Wasp. Their absence is absence of measurement.

Do not quote the numbers in this document as current. The ceilings they are
measured against live in `lang/guest-grammar.edn` under `:admission-limits` and
in each kit's `:qualification`, and those move. Rerun the detector.

## Amendment 2026-09-06 -- a second lens, because the first had a blind spot

The owner asked why Elm beat re-frame and Reagent, then asked for the question
to be recomputed from the substrate -- Kotoba's pure syntax, Unison properties,
IPLD, IPNI, IPFS, CARv2, biscuit -- rather than from what already exists.

The first part has a short answer and it is in the numbers, not in taste.
re-frame loses four axes to Elm: state 5 against 10 (one atom the whole program
holds), dispatch 4 against 10 (handlers registered into a mutable table at load
time), errors 4 against 10 (no failure contract), identity 5 against 9 (macros
are how it is used). Reagent loses on state 3 and authority 3 for one reason: a
ratom is a cell held outside the function that reads it, and the reader is
discovered by dereferencing during render. Elm did not win for being pure. It
won because the state is a value, the message set is sealed, and failure is a
value.

The second part exposed a real defect in the first rubric. Its seven axes
measure fit with the guest language surface as it ships. Not one of them looks
at content addressing, IPLD, IPNI, CARv2 or biscuit -- so it can rank an
architecture that merely compiles above one the substrate was built for, and it
did. A second rubric now scores the same question from the substrate down, and
deliberately does not ask whether an implementation or a kit exists.

scripts/architecture-fit-score.cljs takes --rubric and --dataset, so both lenses
run through one scorer and one validator. What a dataset entry must carry is now
declared by its rubric (:entry-contract), because the two lenses group by
different things and cite different kinds of source. The v1 lens is unchanged
and still totals mean 43.9 -- checked, because a refactor that moves the numbers
is a different change.

### The eight substrate axes

cid-identity (Unison definition CID + IPLD value CID), structural-sharing (IPLD
merkle links), fetch-locality (CARv2 write-locality packs and range reads),
discoverability (IPNI provider records and IPFS retrieval), attenuation
(biscuit), effect-row (abilities, effect rows, the Effect IR), replay (the
Execution IR), pure-surface (the guest grammar, included because the substrate
is only reachable through the language).

### What changed, and what did not

19 entries, mean 50.2, range 14 to 76, median 58.

**The front-end answer changes.** Merkle document UI scores 74; TEA with its
state in memory scores 52. The shape is the same and survives -- state plus
event to next state, view a pure function of state. The representation is what
the substrate replaces: state and view are IPLD values, so the rendered document
has a CID, and two renders are compared by walking down while their CIDs differ.
Neither the full-tree commit ui-v1 does today nor a virtual-DOM differ: the
update set is a property of the data. TEA loses exactly four axes and they are
the four the first lens had no column for -- the model is a value nobody can
name, the view is rebuilt then diffed, everything is bounded by one tab's
memory, and no third party can fetch any of it.

re-frame at 40 is worth reading rather than dismissing: two of its three central
ideas -- effects returned as data, a pure reducer from event to next state --
are exactly right here and score 9 each. The third, one atom the whole program
holds, is what costs it. Replace the cell with a CID and most of re-frame
survives the translation. Reagent at 23 and React at 22 do not, for the same
reason in both cases: the shape of the computation is not a value anywhere.

**The stack that falls out is one answer per layer, and it is coherent rather
than assembled**: event sourcing where one event is one CAR pack (76) for the
durable plane, Merkle-CRDT local-first (75) for convergence, merkle document UI
(74) for the interface, extension by DefCID (74), IPLD selector query (72),
abilities or program-as-value (70) for effects, biscuit attenuation (67) for
authority. They are one system said seven ways: the application is a fold over a
content-addressed log, every intermediate is a CID, the UI is the newest state's
document, authority is a token that only narrows, and discovery is an indexer.

**Discoverability is the axis that separates the field** (mean 5.1, widest
spread at 3.8). Every in-process architecture scores 1 on it, and that single
fact is what reorders the table relative to the first lens.

Datomic-shape is the instructive near-miss: seven axes right, and fetch-locality
2, because the query surface takes a materialised database so cost is
O(database) rather than O(result). That number was measured, not reasoned about.
The IPLD selector reaches 8 on the same axis by making the question name the
subgraph.

### What this does not say

Nothing here is an adoption recommendation. The substrate lens deliberately does
not ask whether an implementation exists, and on that question the first lens is
still the one to read: ui-v1 is what ships, and it commits a 32-node flat tree
against a base revision. A merkle document UI is what to build toward, not what
can be imported this week. The gap between the two lenses for the same
architecture is the size of the work, and saying so is the point of having both.

The break tests were run the same way as for the first lens: an unknown level
reports `merkle-document-ui unknown-level axis=replay`, a removed evidence URL
reports `biscuit-attenuated-request missing-required-field`, and an empty
dataset REFUSES with exit 2 rather than passing.

## Amendment 2026-09-06 (2) -- the closest existing systems, and two investigations

Asked which existing framework in Haskell, Unison, Lisp or elsewhere comes
closest, seven were scored rather than described and are now rows in the
substrate dataset: IPVM with UCAN and WNFS (75), Willow with Meadowcap (70), the
AT Protocol repository (64), Automerge (63), Hypercore and Holepunch (63),
Spritely Goblins (47), self-adjusting computation (26).

The attenuation ladder gained a level while scoring them, and it corrected an
existing row rather than only accommodating a new one. A hypercore read key and
an Erlang pid are both unforgeable references that grant exactly what they grant
and cannot be narrowed, which is neither an attenuable token nor a value whose
level text promises narrowing is possible. `:unforgeable-bearer` at 7 says that,
and actor-otp moved from 9 to 7 because a pid was never narrowable.

Two answers are worth carrying. IPVM reaches the Execution IR from the other
direction -- an invocation names its task, its effects and its authority, and
the receipt is the CID of what happened -- and it is the closest existing system
to the whole stack, at spec maturity and with no interface layer. AT Protocol is
the only entry here running at scale, and it is instructive for the axis it
drops: a repository is a Merkle Search Tree of DAG-CBOR records synced as CAR
with an AppView folding the firehose, and authority is a DID plus an OAuth
session, so nothing attenuates.

### Investigation 1 -- the interface layer. The earlier claim was wrong.

This ADR said the merkle document UI had no close existing implementation and
that the interface was where the novelty lay. That was not measured, and it is
false. It is largely built, in this workspace, and has been since 2026-07-27.

`kotoba-lang/amu` carries nine W4 slices whose exit gate is, in the migration
plan's own words, that one logical UI or document value can be inspected as
data, rendered to an HTML stream, reconciled to browser DOM, hashed
deterministically, and rejected when its resource bounds are exceeded. That
sentence is the requirement list for a merkle document UI, written before this
ADR asked for one.

What is measurably there: thirty-six `document-*` operations callable from guest
source, among them `document-sha256`, `document-canonical-bytes`,
`document-equal?`, `document-assoc`, `document-merge` and `document-print` with
`document-read` as its inverse. `reconcileUiDocument` is real, lives in
`amu/runtime/browser-host.mjs` and is called from `dom-driver.mjs`. Slice 3 has
`document-sha256` producing the same hex under the KIR interpreter, restricted
ESM and real wasm on the browser host. Slice 6 has the pure guest HTML stream
and the host DOM reconcile agreeing byte for byte on one shared document, still
agreeing after a persistent `document-assoc` leaf update, with the digests
differing. Slice 7 makes `document-print` emit the hex of
`document-canonical-bytes` -- the same encoding the digest uses.

So the gap is not the idea and not the primitives. It is the same shape as the
fs and process capabilities: **the language has it and the kit does not.**
`ui-v1` speaks a flat set of at most 32 nodes carrying parent keywords; the
document plane speaks a tree with canonical bytes and a digest. Slice 4 says so
in its own docstring -- it does not replace the flat `:ui/commit` capability
kit, which W5 owns.

One reading hazard found on the way: `document-sha256` is **not** in
`guest-grammar.edn` under `:admitted-builtins`, and it is callable from guest
source regardless. That list is not the authority for the document family.
Do not conclude an operation is absent from its absence there.

### Investigation 2 -- convergence. Also already here, and there are two answers.

`kotoba-lang/crdt` exists: portable CLJC convergent-replica primitives for
Kotoba document editors, pure data and pure functions, no network, no storage,
no actor identity -- hosts own every effect and the package only knows how to
merge. Its README states the gap it fills more precisely than this ADR could:
`kotobase`'s `IStore` gives durable persistence and an append-only log, but
`put`/`get` is last-writer-wins, so two editors racing on one key silently drop
a write; `mst` gives a merkle-diff primitive for comparing state but not for
merging it. Its single runtime dependency is `clock.kotoba`, which is to say the
Lamport rules are themselves written in Kotoba and are what runs.

Its neighbours are registered west projects too: `mst` (AT Protocol Merkle
Search Tree primitives in CLJC), `prolly-tree` (a content-addressed
probabilistic B-tree), `chain` (a parent-linked content-addressed commit chain
whose `prev` is a real tag-42 IPLD link), and `inga` (the consensus plane,
ADR-2608038000).

That last pair is the finding. Convergence here has **two** answers and they are
not the same choice: `crdt` merges without coordination, `inga` orders with a
2f+1 quorum. Which one a given plane takes is a decision to make per plane, not
a gap to fill -- and this ADR should not have implied there was one answer
waiting to be built.

### What this changes about the recommendation

The stack the substrate lens derived is not a proposal. It is an inventory.
`io-ipld`, `io-ipld-car`, `io-ipni-specs`, `mst`, `prolly-tree`, `chain`,
`crdt`, `inga`, `xyz-ucan`, `org-biscuitsec` and `atprotocol` are all registered
west projects and all CLJC, and the document plane in `amu` covers the
interface. What is missing is not the parts. It is the assembly, and the one
named piece of it is W5: the kit that would let the UI layer address the
document plane it already has instead of a flat set of thirty-two nodes."}]
